{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · What only a writer holds","loop":56,"ease":"inout","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.799938,-0.065912026,-0.598084597,0.0967691688]},{"t":0.16,"box":[-0.799938,-0.065912026,-0.598084597,0.0967691688]},{"t":0.19,"box":[-0.524398068,-0.0732292364,-0.208288426,0.0881902753]},{"t":0.33,"box":[-0.524398068,-0.0732292364,-0.208288426,0.0881902753]},{"t":0.36,"box":[-0.127636364,-0.0668571429,0.0855584416,0.0715675325]},{"t":0.5,"box":[-0.127636364,-0.0668571429,0.0855584416,0.0715675325]},{"t":0.53,"box":[0.14025974,-0.0683766234,0.308571429,0.0841558442]},{"t":0.67,"box":[0.14025974,-0.0683766234,0.308571429,0.0841558442]},{"t":0.7,"box":[0.416524675,-0.0813974026,0.565293506,0.109916883]},{"t":0.84,"box":[0.416524675,-0.0813974026,0.565293506,0.109916883]},{"t":0.87,"box":[0.645811948,-0.112579481,0.856837403,0.0180841558]}],"stops":[0.16,0.33,0.5,0.67,0.84],"beats":[{"title":"Bernoulli's bank","text":"Lend one coin at a hundred per cent a year. Paid [once](#lit:3), it gives 2; twice, 2.25; monthly, 2.6130; daily, 2.7146; hourly, 2.71813; [every minute](#lit:8), 2.718279. [The amounts climb](#lit:2) and the climb slows, closing on [a number no count of payments reaches](#lit:1): e, 2.71828…\n\nEvery step is a fraction, (n + 1)ⁿ/nⁿ, whatever the count. To have e you must have made every payment there could be: it is the end of a process that does not end."},{"title":"π completes the turn","text":"A reader composes directions by tangent addition, in fractions. [One fifth, four times over](#lit:12,13,14,15), reads 1/5, 5/12, 37/55, 120/119; take back one part in 239 and it lands [on the corner](#lit:17,18), exactly 1. Machin's identity among directions is finite, and the reader's.\n\nThe corner's angle is π/4, and π/4 is [1 − 1/3 + 1/5 − 1/7 + …](#lit:21): every partial sum a fraction, five terms 0.8349, a thousand within a two-thousandth, none of them [π/4](#lit:20). The number is the sum finished, and finishing it is the writer's."},{"title":"The turn comes back, the scale goes on","text":"The reader's ignorance is two groups, turns and rescalings. [A turn](#lit:23) by a whole revolution is no turn at all: its exponential map, e^(iθ), has a period, [2π](#lit:22). π is the measure of the turn closing.\n\n[A rescaling](#lit:25) never comes back: twice, twice again, never home. Its map, κ = eᵗ, has no period, only a base, and e is the base that counts growth at a rate of one. π says the turn returns; e says how the scale goes on."},{"title":"Where they meet","text":"Compound the turn as Bernoulli compounded the coin: (1 + iπ/n)ⁿ, [n steps](#lit:32) of a fraction of a half turn each. One step overshoots far up; two, four, eight, sixteen, sixty-four close in on [−1](#lit:31), every stage finitely many pieces.\n\nOnly the limit lies on [the writer's arc](#lit:29): e^(iπ) = −1, the scale's base raised to the turn's half total. The reader holds that place as a pair, the horizon [0 : 1], with nothing infinite in it; Lindemann's proof that π is not algebraic goes through this point."},{"title":"Met, not made","text":"Transcendental numbers are in the reader's world, and the reader meets them. [The direction tan 1](#lit:38,39), 1.5574…, is a direction like any other: paint arrives along it, and it sits in order between [3/2 and 8/5](#lit:36,37).\n\nA thing e times the thumb away reads [one in e](#lit:42), between [one in two and one in four](#lit:41), held there by order. The reader holds the direction and the reading; 1.5574… and \"e times\" are the writer's account of where they are."},{"title":"A machine holds fractions","text":"Every floating-point number is an integer over a power of two. The machine's π is [884279719003555/2⁴⁸](#lit:49); [the doubles near it](#lit:48) are 2⁻⁵¹ apart, and [π](#lit:50) falls 0.276 of the way to the next one. Running Machin's series for a billion digits, the machine holds a fraction at every moment, as the reader does.\n\nAsked for the writer's numbers it keeps their names, pi and E, and proves exp(iπ) = −1 by rules about the names. The turn comes back and the scale goes on, and to say either in a number you have to have held all of it. [The essay](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/15-what-only-a-writer-holds.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.222077922,0.222077922,-0.222077922,-0.222077922],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":1},{"morph":2},{"morph":3},{"morph":4},{"morph":5},{"morph":6},{"morph":7},{"morph":8},{"morph":9},{"morph":10},{"morph":12},{"morph":13},{"morph":14},{"morph":15},{"morph":16},{"morph":17},{"morph":18},{"morph":11},{"morph":19},{"morph":20},{"morph":21},{"morph":22},{"morph":23},{"morph":25},{"morph":26},{"morph":24},{"morph":27},{"morph":28},{"morph":29},{"morph":32},{"morph":30},{"morph":31},{"morph":33},{"morph":34},{"morph":36},{"morph":37},{"morph":38},{"morph":39},{"morph":35},{"morph":40},{"morph":41},{"morph":42},{"morph":43},{"morph":44},{"morph":46},{"morph":47},{"morph":48},{"morph":49},{"morph":45},{"morph":50},{"morph":51},{"morph":52},{"morph":57},{"morph":58},{"morph":53},{"morph":59},{"morph":54},{"morph":60},{"morph":55},{"morph":61},{"morph":62},{"morph":63},{"morph":56}],"morphs":[{"exact":true,"open":true,"holes":null,"keys":[{"x":[-0.783116883,-0.783116883,-0.621116883],"y":[0.0799480519,-0.0490909091,-0.0490909091],"r":[0,0,0],"c":[null,null,null]}],"fill":null,"inPlace":true,"lag":0,"fx":null,"own":{"parent":-1,"parentAt":null,"follow":null,"look":-1,"lookRef":0,"phase":0,"rate":1,"spin":0,"motion":null,"onto":null,"laps":1,"start":null,"times":null,"clip":{"start":0.004,"len":0.986,"show":"","in":{"kind":"draw","len":0.01}},"eases":null,"straight":false,"trail":null,"cols":null,"path":null},"stroke":"#9a927f","width":2,"strokeOpacity":1},{"exact":true,"open":true,"holes":n